  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "At home, Nastya tells Kostya to close his eyes and open his mouth so she can swab his cheek for the DNA test. Back at the hotel, Mikhail warns Viktor Petrovich that Patrick Malouda is arriving—only to discover that someone stole Barinov's wheelchair!",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Костик, закрой глаза и открой рот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем мне открывать сейчас рот?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Так нужно, давай быстрее открывай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Настя, это что, такая шутка?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Витя, инспектор мне уже звонил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он совсем скоро будет здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Так что давай готовь коляску.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, а где моя коляска?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Как вы могли потерять коляску?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Эй, стой на месте сейчас же!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Куда ты на ней едешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ну что ты там делаешь?",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Костик, закрой глаза и открой рот.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Kostik, close your eyes and open your mouth.' Notice how Russian expresses 'close' and 'mouth' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Зачем мне открывать сейчас рот?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Why should I open my mouth right now?' The meaning hinges on 'why' working together with 'mouth' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Так нужно, давай быстрее открывай.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is necessary, come on, open up quickly.' Nastya brings out the contrast between 'so' and 'needed' in a few short words. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Настя, это что, такая шутка?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Nastya, is this some kind of joke?' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'Nastya' and 'this' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Витя, инспектор мне уже звонил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Vitya, the inspector already called me.' In Russian, the idea of 'inspector' flows naturally into 'called' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Он совсем скоро будет здесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He will be here very soon.' The meaning hinges on 'he' working together with 'here' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Так что давай готовь коляску.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So come on, get the wheelchair ready.' In Russian, the idea of 'so' flows naturally into 'wheelchair' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, а где моя коляска?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, where is my wheelchair?' The grammar packs 'wait' and 'wheelchair' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Как вы могли потерять коляску?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'How could you lose the wheelchair?' Mikhail chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'wheelchair' to keep a polite distance. Chef Barinov answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Эй, стой на месте сейчас же!",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hey, stop right there this instant!' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'hey' before landing on 'but'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Куда ты на ней едешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Where are you riding off to on it?' Chef Barinov addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'are driving'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ну что ты там делаешь?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, what are you doing over there?' The meaning hinges on 'come on' working together with 'are doing' in spoken Russian. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
